            Micellar casein protein powder is the slow-digesting protein in milk, separated by filtration so its natural structure survives, and a typical scoop gives 24 to 26 g of protein. It is a sensible choice for a protein serving you will go several hours without eating after, such as before bed, but it does not build more muscle than whey when total daily protein is the same. Of our five picks, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Casein is the best all-rounder, Naked Casein is the cleanest label, and BulkSupplements is the only one we confirmed in the NSF Certified for Sport registry.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24-26 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop across our picks</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1.17</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Lowest cost per 25 g we saw</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~80%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Share of cow's milk protein that is casein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">30-40 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pre-sleep dose used in most studies</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you want the full ranked list with more brands, including Transparent Labs and NOW, our pillar guide to the <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> covers six products. This page goes deeper on the micellar question itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Five Micellar Casein Powders Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ranked on what the label says (protein per scoop, ingredients, sweeteners), on what the brand or a certifier says about testing, and on cost per 25 g of protein at the Amazon price we saw on 20 November 2026. We did not taste or test these products; flavor and mixing notes are buyer reports.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-micellar-casein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Micellar Casein Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Micellar casein is casein protein kept in the form it takes in milk: tiny clusters, called micelles, held together by calcium phosphate. About 80% of the protein in cow's milk is casein and about 20% is whey. In milk, the casein floats in these micelles, which is part of why milk looks white rather than clear. A micellar casein powder is made by passing skim milk through very fine membranes (microfiltration and ultrafiltration) that let water, lactose, minerals and the small whey proteins through while holding the bigger micelles back. The retained fraction is then dried into powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the process is mostly physical, without strong acids or heat treatments that break the structure apart, the micelles stay largely intact. That matters for what happens after you drink it. In the acidic environment of the stomach, intact micelles clot into a soft gel, much like milk curdling when you make cheese. That gel empties from the stomach slowly, so amino acids trickle into the blood over several hours instead of arriving in one spike.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a label, you will see the ingredient written as "micellar casein". Some products add a qualifier: Optimum Nutrition prints "100% protein from micellar casein", and Dymatize says 100% of the casein in Elite Casein is micellar. That wording is worth reading closely, because a product described as "casein protein" or "casein blend" may instead use calcium caseinate, sodium caseinate or a mix. Our BulkSupplements pick is an example: the brand lists the single ingredient as "Casein Protein" and does not say which form.
          </p>

          <p>
            Micellar casein powders are naturally thick. The same gelling that slows digestion also makes the powder absorb more liquid than whey, which is why buyers so often use it to make protein pudding, overnight oats or a frosting-like topping. It also means a casein shake made with the same water as a whey shake will usually be noticeably thicker, and some buyers report it clumps unless it goes into the liquid gradually or into a blender.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nutritionally, micellar casein is a complete protein with all nine essential amino acids. It is a little lower in leucine than whey per gram: Dymatize publishes 2.3 g of leucine per 25 g of Elite Casein protein, while whey isolates often land around 2.5 to 2.8 g per 25 g. It is also rich in calcium, since the micelles carry calcium phosphate with them; the labels here that list calcium show 500 to 580 mg per serving.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="micellar-vs-caseinate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Micellar Casein vs Calcium Caseinate: What Is the Difference?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Micellar casein keeps the native micelle structure, while calcium caseinate is casein that has been precipitated with acid and then neutralized with a calcium compound, which breaks the micelles apart. Both are casein and both are complete proteins with similar amino acid profiles. The difference is the physical form, and that changes how the powder behaves in a glass and, to some degree, in the gut.
          </p>

          <p>
            To make a caseinate, manufacturers add acid to skim milk until the casein falls out of solution at its isoelectric point, around pH 4.6. The curd is washed and then dissolved again with an alkali: calcium hydroxide gives calcium caseinate, sodium hydroxide gives sodium caseinate. The result dissolves easily, emulsifies fat well and is cheap, which is why caseinates appear in so many protein blends, meal replacement shakes, coffee creamers and processed foods. Check the ingredient list of a ready-to-drink shake and you will often find sodium or calcium caseinate near the top.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Feature</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Micellar casein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calcium caseinate</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">How it is made</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Membrane filtration of skim milk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acid precipitation, then neutralized with calcium</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Micelle structure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Largely intact</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Disrupted</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">In water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thick, gels, can clump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dissolves more easily, thinner</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Where you see it</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dedicated casein powders</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Blends, creamers, meal shakes, bars</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Does the form change digestion speed? The honest answer is that it probably does a little, but the evidence is thinner than supplement marketing suggests. The slow, clotting behavior described in the classic studies was measured with intact casein, and the micelle is what forms the gel. Caseinates still coagulate in the stomach, but the curd tends to be looser. There are few head-to-head human trials comparing micellar casein with calcium caseinate on muscle protein synthesis, so we would not claim one builds more muscle than the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, the difference that matters most to a buyer is what you are paying for. If a tub is sold as a casein powder, priced like a casein powder and taken specifically for slow release, it should say micellar casein on the label. If it lists calcium caseinate or a "protein blend" with casein somewhere in it, you are getting a cheaper, more soluble ingredient that may digest a bit faster. Neither is unsafe; just make sure the label matches the claim on the front.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">LABEL CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Look for the word micellar in the ingredient list, not just on the front.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">"Casein protein", "milk protein blend" and "time-release protein" can all hide caseinates. If a brand uses micellar casein, it almost always says so, because it is the more expensive ingredient.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="micellar-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Micellar Casein Better Than Whey?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Micellar casein is not better than whey overall; it digests more slowly, while whey digests faster and triggers a larger short-term rise in muscle protein synthesis. Which one suits you depends on when you take it, not on which is the superior protein. For a direct, side-by-side breakdown, see our <a href="/casein-vs-whey-protein-powder/">casein vs whey protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            The slow-versus-fast idea comes from a 1997 study by Boirie and colleagues in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. Using labeled amino acids, they found whey produced a fast, high and short-lived rise in blood amino acids, while casein produced a lower, flatter rise that lasted several hours. They called whey a "fast" protein and casein a "slow" one, and the labels stuck. Casein also reduced whole-body protein breakdown more, while whey stimulated synthesis more in the short term.
          </p>

          <p>
            Later work focused on muscle specifically. A 2009 study by Tang and colleagues in the Journal of Applied Physiology compared whey hydrolysate, soy and micellar casein at rest and after resistance exercise in young men. Whey produced the highest muscle protein synthesis over the three hours measured, and casein the lowest. Part of the explanation is leucine: whey reaches the blood faster and contains more of it, and leucine acts as a signal that switches synthesis on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those are short-term measurements, though. Over weeks and months, what predicts muscle gain is mostly total daily protein and training, not the type of protein in any single shake. Systematic reviews of protein supplementation have found that once daily intake reaches roughly 1.6 g per kg of body weight, extra protein adds little, and differences between dairy protein types shrink. Whey and casein both work; the choice is mainly about timing, texture and price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pick micellar casein if, pick whey if</h3>

          <p>
            Pick micellar casein if your protein shake comes before a long gap without food, such as bedtime or a long workday, if you like a thick shake or pudding, or if a slower-digesting snack keeps you fuller. Pick whey if you want a protein right after training, a thinner shake that mixes in seconds, or the most leucine per gram. Plenty of people use both: whey after workouts and casein at night. If you mostly want whey, our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide is the place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-bed-evidence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Does Micellar Casein Before Bed Actually Work?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Taking 30 to 40 g of casein before bed raises overnight muscle protein synthesis in studies, but whether it builds more muscle than eating the same protein earlier in the day is not settled. That is the honest summary, and it is a more modest claim than most tubs make.
          </p>

          <p>
            The key acute study is Res and colleagues, published in Medicine and Science in Sports and Exercise in 2012. Young men did an evening resistance workout and then drank either 40 g of casein or a placebo 30 minutes before sleep. The casein was digested and absorbed normally overnight, and whole-body protein balance and overnight muscle protein synthesis were higher with casein. Similar results followed in older men, using 40 g of casein, from the same research group in Maastricht.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best-known long-term trial is Snijders and colleagues in the Journal of Nutrition in 2015. Young men trained for 12 weeks and took either a protein supplement with 27.5 g of casein before bed or a non-caloric placebo. The protein group gained more strength and more muscle size. The catch is that the protein group also ate more total protein each day, so the study cannot separate the effect of timing from the effect of simply eating more protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Studies that matched daily protein, giving the same amount either before bed or earlier, have generally found small or no differences in muscle gain. The International Society of Sports Nutrition's 2017 position stand on protein and exercise concludes that 30 to 40 g of casein before sleep can increase overnight muscle protein synthesis and metabolic rate without blunting overnight fat breakdown, while treating total daily protein as the main driver. So the practical reading is this: casein at night is a convenient way to reach your daily target, and it may give a small extra edge, but it is not a requirement.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one more practical point. If you already eat a protein-rich dinner a couple of hours before bed, a casein shake adds less. It earns its place for people who train in the evening, eat dinner early, or struggle to hit their protein goal. For a wider look at night-time options, including milk and Greek yogurt, see our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-before-bed/">best protein powder before bed</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

