          <p>
            These programs check different things. NSF Certified for Sport screens for hundreds of substances banned in sport, confirms the label claims and audits the manufacturing facility, and it is the standard most US professional leagues recognize. Informed Choice is a banned-substance program run by LGC. NSF Contents Certified checks that what is in the container matches the label, which is useful for accuracy but is not a banned-substance screen. "Third-party tested" with no program named tells you a lab was involved but not what it tested for or how often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certifications can lapse or apply only to some flavors or sizes, so check the registry yourself before relying on one. Search the product on the NSF Certified for Sport site or the Informed Choice site, and match the flavor and size to your tub. For drug-tested athletes, a registry listing is the only claim worth relying on.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of these brands publish heavy-metal test results for their casein on the product page as far as we could find. The Nutricost and BulkSupplements pages carry California Proposition 65 warnings for lead, which signals that a product may exceed California's very low threshold but is not itself a test result. Dairy proteins generally test lower for heavy metals than many plant proteins, but if this matters to you, ask the brand for a certificate of analysis.
