          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every casein powder is micellar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Front labels often say casein while the ingredient list shows calcium caseinate, sodium caseinate or a blend. Those are cheaper and dissolve more easily, but they are not the same ingredient. If slow release is why you are buying, read the ingredient list and look for the word micellar before paying casein prices.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking one small scoop and expecting research results</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The bedtime studies mostly used 30 to 40 g of casein protein, not the 24 g in a typical single scoop. One scoop still counts toward your daily protein, but if you are copying the research protocol you will need one and a half to two scoops, and your tub will run out faster.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using casein as a post-workout shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Casein digests slowly and produced a smaller short-term rise in muscle protein synthesis than whey after exercise in the Tang 2009 study. It is not harmful after training, and total daily protein matters most, but if you want one shake right after a workout, whey is the better fit for the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a vague testing claim</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Third-party tested with no named program tells you little. Only a listing in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice registry confirms banned-substance screening. Drug-tested athletes should search the registry for the exact flavor and size before buying, rather than relying on a front-label badge or an Amazon bullet point.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing it like whey and giving up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Micellar casein thickens and clumps if you dump a scoop into a little water and shake. Add the powder gradually to cold liquid, use more liquid than you would for whey, or blend it. For pudding, use much less liquid and let it sit for a few minutes to set.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
